    id: 'rp7', title: "仔细阅读 · 大学录取还要不要看考试", minutes: 12,
    text: `For most of the past century, American students applying to selective colleges have faced a familiar ordeal: a standardized test taken on a Saturday morning, whose score would follow them through the admissions process. During the pandemic, when testing centers closed, hundreds of colleges stopped requiring the scores. Many kept the policy afterward, describing it as a step toward fairness. Now several prominent universities have reversed course and brought the requirement back, reopening a debate that many thought had been settled.
The case against testing has always rested on inequality. Students from wealthy families can afford private tutoring and multiple attempts, and their scores reflect those advantages as much as their ability. Dropping the requirement, supporters argued, would encourage talented students from poorer backgrounds to apply to colleges they had once considered out of reach. In the first years of the test-optional era, applications from such students did rise.
What happened next surprised many observers. Researchers at several universities examined how admitted students actually performed once enrolled, and found that test scores predicted college grades better than high school grades did. The reason, they suggested, is that high school grades have risen steadily for decades, so that a top grade now distinguishes one student from another far less than it once did. A test taken by everyone under the same conditions provides a common measure that grades no longer offer.
More strikingly, the researchers argued that the test-optional policy may have hurt the very students it was meant to help. Applicants from disadvantaged schools often chose not to submit scores that would in fact have counted in their favor, since admissions officers judge results in the context of a student's background. A score that looks ordinary in a wealthy suburb may be exceptional at a school where few students go on to college. Without it, officers had less evidence with which to identify such applicants.
None of this means the critics were wrong about inequality. Test scores do track family income, and no test can measure qualities such as persistence or curiosity. But the alternatives are not free of bias either. Essays can be polished by paid consultants, and impressive extracurricular activities often require money and free time that poorer students lack. Every measure of merit, it seems, reflects some degree of privilege.
The more useful question, then, may not be whether to use tests but how. A score is one piece of information, not a verdict, and its value depends on whether those reading it understand where a student started. Colleges that treat it this way may find that the test, long criticized as a barrier, can also serve as a ladder.`,
