    id: 'rp8', title: "仔细阅读 · 在线课程为何难坚持", minutes: 12,
    text: `When the first massive open online courses appeared around 2012, they were greeted with extraordinary enthusiasm. Lectures by professors at elite universities would be available to anyone with an internet connection, free of charge. Commentators predicted that traditional campuses would struggle to survive, and that a student in a remote village would soon receive the same education as one at a famous university. A decade later, those predictions look premature.
The most widely reported problem is completion. Of the many people who sign up for a typical online course, only a small fraction, often fewer than one in ten, reach the end. Defenders point out that this figure is misleading, since many people enroll out of curiosity with no intention of finishing, much as a reader might browse a few chapters of a library book. Yet even among learners who say at the start that they intend to complete a course, most do not.
A second disappointment concerns who benefits. The founders of online courses hoped to reach people who lacked access to higher education. In practice, the typical participant already holds a university degree and lives in a wealthy country. Online learning, it turns out, works best for those who have already learned how to learn: people with strong reading skills, a quiet place to study and the habit of managing their own time.
Why is studying alone so hard? Research on motivation offers part of the answer. Traditional education surrounds students with structures they rarely notice: fixed timetables, classmates who expect them to turn up, teachers who notice when they fall behind. These structures do not merely deliver information; they create a sense of obligation. Remove them, and even highly motivated learners find that other demands on their time take priority.
Course designers have learned from these findings. Many courses now break material into short units with regular deadlines, group learners into small online teams and send reminders when a student has been inactive. Some charge a modest fee for a certificate, which appears to raise completion rates considerably, perhaps because people value what they have paid for. Others have been absorbed into university degree programs, where online lessons are combined with assessment and personal support.
The result is less revolutionary than the early promises but arguably more useful. Online courses have not replaced universities; instead, they have become one of the tools that universities and employers use. For a working adult who wants to learn a new skill, they remain a remarkable resource. Their history is a reminder that access to knowledge is not the same as education, and that the human side of teaching was never as easy to digitize as it seemed.`,
