    id: 'rp9', title: "仔细阅读 · 基因编辑的边界", minutes: 12,
    text: `In 2023, regulators in Britain and the United States approved the first medical treatment based on CRISPR, a technique that allows scientists to cut and alter DNA with remarkable precision. The treatment targets sickle cell disease, an inherited blood disorder that causes severe pain and shortens lives. Patients' own blood-producing cells are removed, edited in a laboratory to switch on a form of hemoglobin normally active only before birth, and then returned to the body. In clinical trials, almost all patients were freed from the painful crises that had dominated their lives.
It is hard to overstate how quickly this happened. CRISPR was described as a gene-editing tool only in 2012. In just over a decade it moved from academic papers to approved medicine, a pace almost unheard of in biology. Researchers are now testing it against inherited forms of blindness, high cholesterol and certain cancers.
Yet the excitement is accompanied by caution, and not only about safety. Editing the cells of an adult patient affects only that person. Editing an embryo, by contrast, produces changes that would be passed on to future generations. In 2018 a Chinese scientist announced that he had done exactly this, creating twin girls whose genes he had altered in an attempt to make them resistant to HIV. The response from the scientific community was almost universal condemnation, and he was later sentenced to prison. Most countries now prohibit such heritable editing, at least for the time being.
The objections are partly practical. Editing is precise but not perfect, and changes made in the wrong place could cause harm that might appear only years later, in people who never agreed to take part in an experiment. But the deeper concerns are ethical. If editing could be used to prevent disease, it might also be used to select for height, intelligence or appearance, and such enhancements would probably be available only to the rich. Critics fear a future in which inequality is written into biology itself.
A further problem is cost. The approved sickle cell treatment is priced at around two million dollars per patient. Most people with the disease live in sub-Saharan Africa and India, where such sums are unimaginable. Unless the price falls dramatically, a cure developed partly through research on these populations may remain out of their reach.
None of these difficulties is a reason to abandon the technology. They are, however, a reason to think carefully about who decides how it is used. Scientists alone cannot settle questions about which human traits are worth changing, and markets alone will not ensure that cures reach those who need them most.`,
