    id: 'rp10', title: "仔细阅读 · 太空经济的繁荣与隐忧", minutes: 12,
    text: `For most of the space age, going into orbit was something only governments could afford. Rockets were built for national agencies, used once and then left to fall into the ocean, and each launch cost hundreds of millions of dollars. Over the past fifteen years that picture has changed dramatically. Private companies now launch most of the world's satellites, and reusable rockets that land upright after delivering their cargo have cut the cost of reaching orbit to a fraction of what it once was.
The effects of cheaper access are already visible on the ground. Thousands of small satellites now provide broadband internet to ships, aircraft and remote villages that cable networks will never reach. Others photograph the entire surface of the Earth every day, allowing farmers to monitor crops, insurers to assess flood damage and researchers to track illegal logging almost as it happens. Economists estimate that the global space economy is now worth more than half a trillion dollars a year, most of it generated by services that ordinary people use without ever thinking of space.
Enthusiasts see this as only the beginning. They speak of factories in orbit producing materials that are difficult to make under gravity, of mining asteroids for rare metals and of holidays on private space stations. Some of these ideas may eventually prove profitable. Others rest on assumptions about demand and cost that have not yet been tested, and history offers plenty of examples of technologies that were possible long before they were worthwhile.
Meanwhile, success is creating problems of its own. The number of active satellites has multiplied in just a few years, and each launch adds to a growing cloud of debris, from dead satellites to flakes of paint. Because objects in orbit travel at enormous speeds, even a small piece can destroy a satellite on impact, producing thousands of new fragments in turn. Scientists warn that, in the worst case, certain orbits could become too crowded to use safely.
Astronomers have raised a different complaint. Bright satellites crossing the night sky leave streaks across telescope images, interfering with observations that can take years to plan. Companies have experimented with darker coatings, but the number of satellites planned for the coming decade is so large that the problem seems certain to grow.
What is missing is not technology but rules. The main international treaty on space was written in 1967, when only two countries could reach orbit, and it says little about commercial activity, debris or traffic management. Space belongs to everyone, yet no single authority manages it. Unless nations agree on how to share it, the new space economy risks repeating a familiar pattern on Earth: a common resource exploited quickly by a few, at a cost borne by all.`,
