    id: 'rp11', title: "仔细阅读 · 社交媒体上的比较陷阱", minutes: 12,
    text: `Scroll through any social media feed and you will find a steady parade of other people's best moments: holidays on white beaches, promotions at work, perfectly arranged dinners, children holding prizes. Few people post photographs of their unpaid bills or their arguments with their partners. The result is a version of reality in which everyone else seems to be doing slightly better than you are.
Psychologists have long known that people judge themselves largely by comparison with others. In the 1950s, the social psychologist Leon Festinger proposed that humans have a basic drive to evaluate their abilities and opinions, and that when no objective standard is available, they look to the people around them. Such comparisons are not necessarily harmful. Looking at someone slightly more skilled can inspire us to improve, and noticing that others share our difficulties can be comforting.
The trouble with online comparison lies in its scale and its selection. In the past, a person compared themselves mainly with neighbors, classmates and colleagues, people whose lives they knew in some detail, flaws included. Today the comparison group may include thousands of people, many of them strangers, presented through carefully chosen images. Measured against such a standard, almost anyone's ordinary life will look disappointing.
Research tends to support this concern, though with important qualifications. Studies have found that people who spend more time comparing themselves with others online report lower self-esteem and more symptoms of depression. Yet the effect depends heavily on how social media is used. Actively messaging friends and taking part in conversations seems to do little harm, and may even help. It is passive scrolling, watching other lives without interacting, that is most closely linked to feeling worse.
Interestingly, comparison does not only make people feel inferior. Researchers distinguish between upward comparison, with people who seem better off, and downward comparison, with those who seem worse off. Downward comparison can bring temporary relief, but it is a fragile kind of comfort, since it depends on someone else's misfortune, and it does little for lasting wellbeing.
What can individuals do? Simply deleting social media apps is one option, and experiments in which participants stopped using them for a few weeks have found modest improvements in mood. For those who stay, psychologists recommend more deliberate habits: following accounts that inform or amuse rather than impress, reminding oneself that a post is an edited highlight, and spending more time interacting than watching. The aim is not to stop comparing altogether, which may be impossible, but to choose more carefully what we compare ourselves with.`,
